course: dictionary
lesson: 9491
title: Steelmanning the other side
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When you argue, it is tempting to make the other side sound silly, so it is easy to knock down. A fairer way is to steelman: describe the other side's argument in its strongest, fairest form before you answer it, even if you disagree.
explain: «Strong arguers steelman: they say the other idea back in its best, strongest words before answering it.» Steelman is a verb, so you can steelman, steelmans, steelmanned or steelmanning an argument. Its opposite is a straw man: a weak, twisted version of someone's view that is easy to knock over.
explain: Steelmanning does not mean agreeing. «A steelman means describing the other side's view in its strongest form; it helps you understand without making you agree.» Look for common ground, the things both sides want, and then explain why you still think you are right.
fact: To steelman an argument means to describe it in its strongest, fairest form.
fact: A steelman is that strongest version of the other side's argument.
fact: A straw man is a weak, twisted version of an argument, made up so it is easy to knock down.
fact: «Steelmanning each other's best reason before arguing helps two people understand each other and find a middle way.»
fact: Steelman can also be a noun, but this is rare and old: a man who works making steel.
exercise: «Steelmanning means saying the other person's idea in its strongest, fairest way.» What does steelmanning mean? | *Describing the other side's idea in its strongest, fairest form | Saying nothing until the debate ends | Making the other side's idea sound silly | Agreeing with everything the other side says
exercise: Mia says, «I'll steelman Sam's point before I answer it.» What is Mia going to do? | *Describe Sam's point in its best form before she answers | Make Sam's point sound silly | Shout over Sam until he stops
exercise: Which word is the opposite of steelman? | Common ground | *Straw man | Agreement | Summary
test: Which phrase means the most helpful, fair version of someone's argument? | A version they never said | *Their strongest, fairest form | Their weakest excuse | The shortest possible version
test: «Steelmanning helps two people find a middle way.» Why does this help? | *They understand each other and find common ground | They stop talking altogether | They pretend the other person is right | They win the argument quickly
exam: Ravi steelmans his opponent's argument, then says, «I still disagree, but here is why.» What did Ravi show? | Weak thinking and a quick win | *Respect and clear thinking, without having to agree | Nothing at all | Agreement with everything his opponent said
